//
// The `vector<single>` half of loft#899, which its sibling guard
// `899-unbound-file-read-temp.loft` structurally CANNOT hold.
//
// The wrong value came from the read's temp having no `main_vector<T>` wrapper
// registered, and ANY other `vector<T>` local in the file registers one — including
// the control row a good test wants, and including the parameter of a helper the test
// calls. So the moment that guard added `v = f#read(…)` as its control, it also fixed
// the very cell it was pinning: on the broken compiler it passes every `vector<single>`
// row and fails only on `vector<P>`.
//
// This file therefore declares NO other vector of any kind: the read below is the only
// one in it, its element type is `single`, and it is consumed with no binding. That is
// the filed repro exactly, and it answered `len 1` — the header of an untyped store,
// read one element in.
//
// Keep it minimal. A control row, a helper taking a vector, a second element type —
// each would re-register the wrapper and quietly retire the guard.

fn main() {
  { f = file("r899c.bin"); f#format = LittleEndian; f += 1.5f; f += 2.5f; }
  { f = file("r899c.bin"); f#format = LittleEndian;
    n = len(f#read(8) as vector<single>);
    assert(n == 2, "len = {n}, want 2"); }
  delete("r899c.bin");
  println("ok");
}
